 "note": {
  "status": "draft",
  "text": "A spreadsheet program (Microsoft Excel, LibreOffice Calc, Google Sheets) organises data in a grid of rows and columns, and does calculations.\n• Columns are labelled with letters (A, B, C…), rows with numbers (1, 2, 3…).\n• A cell is where a column and a row meet; its address is the column letter and row number, for example B{3}.\n• A range is a block of cells, for example A{1}:A{5}.\n• A cell can hold text (labels), numbers or a formula.\n• A formula starts with =, for example =A{1}+A{2} or =B{2}*C{2}.\n• Functions are ready-made formulas: =SUM(A{1}:A{5}) adds, =AVERAGE(A{1}:A{5}) finds the mean, =MAX and =MIN find the largest and smallest.\nA file is a workbook; it contains worksheets (sheets).",
  "checks": []
 },
 "worked_example": {
  "status": "draft",
  "vars": {
   "a": 12,
   "b": 15,
   "c": 9
  },
  "problem": "Cells A{1}, A{2} and A{3} contain {a}, {b} and {c}. What does =SUM(A{1}:A{3}) give?",
  "steps": [
   "SUM adds all the cells in the range A{1} to A{3}.",
   "{a} + {b} + {c} = {a + b + c}."
  ],
  "answer": "{a + b + c}",
  "checks": []
 },
